<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="lab10">
  <title>Lab 10</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      This lab is intended to practice ideas related to vector spaces and inner products. It is an opportunity
      to explore important concepts with others, explain your understanding, and ask questions. As such, these exercises should
      be thought of as a starting point for exploration, and you should feel free to ask related questions or explore related ideas
      if your interest is led somewhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will be graded on effort and engagement during class time, not necessarily on completing every exercise. Guidelines
      for working together may be provided in class.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
<page>
  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the <m>2</m>-dimensional vector space <m>\C^2</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Considering the dimension of the space, what must the scalar field be? What is the standard basis of this space?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write down two vectors from this space, and decompose them as a linear combination of standard basis
                vectors. At least one of your vectors should have coordinates with non-zero imaginary part.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                This is a Hilbert space when you include the inner product
                <md>
                    \langle \zvec_1, \zvec_2\rangle = \zvec_1 \cdot \overline{\zvec_2}
                </md>, 
                where the above product is the dot product.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Verify the standard basis vectors are normalized. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            A general vector has the form 
                            <m>c_1 \mathbf{e}_1 + c_2\mathbf{e}_2</m>, <m>c_1,c_2 \in \C</m>. Find a condition on <m>c_1,c_2</m> which guarantees the 
                            vector will be normalized.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               Normalize the vector <m>\wvec = 0.5 \mathbf{e}_1 + 0.25i\mathbf{e}_2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    
</exercise>
</page>


</worksheet>
